    document.getElementById("container").innerHTML='<div class="background-container-GPW-PwP"><div class="black"><div class="fade-out-simple"></div><p class="hidden-text-left" style="transform:translate(0,-40px);">The existence of the Peace of Callias, a purported treaty between Athens and Persia, is debated among ancient sources and modern historians. While some ancient historians like Diodorus claim its existence, others like Callisthenes and Theopompus dispute it. Modern scholars also hold differing opinions, with some, like Fine, accepting its possibility, while others, like Sealey, reject it. The terms of the treaty allegedly included granting autonomy to Greek cities in Asia Minor, restricting Persian military movements, and prohibiting Athenian military intervention in Persian territories. These terms, while not overtly humiliating for Persia, acknowledge Athens naval supremacy and seek to maintain peace in the region. However, the absence of clear evidence and Thucydides silence on the matter add to the debates complexity.</p><div class="fade-in-simple" style="transform:translate(0,-89px);"></div></div></div><div class="main-container"></div>'
