    document.getElementById("container").innerHTML='<div class="background-container-GPW-Aftermath"><div class="black"><div class="fade-out-simple"></div><p class="hidden-text-left" style="transform:translate(0,-110px);">Following the conflict with Persia, the Delian League evolved into the Athenian Empire, with Athens retaining control over its allies despite the end of hostilities. Meanwhile, the First Peloponnesian War between Athens and Sparta ended in 445 BC with a thirty-year truce, but tensions persisted, leading to the outbreak of the Second Peloponnesian War in 431 BC. This prolonged conflict ultimately resulted in the downfall of Athenian power and the establishment of Spartan hegemony over Greece, weakening the entire region. In the aftermath, Persia adopted a divide-and-rule strategy, manipulating Athens and Sparta to keep them occupied with internal conflicts, thus avoiding direct confrontation with the Greeks. Open conflict between Greece and Persia didnt resume until 396 BC when Sparta briefly invaded Asia Minor. Subsequent conflicts, like the Corinthian War, saw Athens siding with Persia, leading to further turmoil in Greece. The Kings Peace of 387 BC, forced Sparta to return control of Asian Greek cities in exchange for Persian aid, undoing the gains made by the Greeks against Persia and marking a shift in Greek sentiment towards the earlier Peace of Callias as a symbol of freedom from Persian rule.</p><div class="fade-in-simple" style="transform:translate(0,-170px);"></div></div></div><div class="main-container"></div>'
